The decisive paper facts are now clear: the native task is minimum directed \(k\)-spanner, and the paper explicitly supplies polynomial-time approximation—not an exact witness extractor. For unit lengths and constant \(k\), its flow LP is polynomial-size and exactly solvable; for general lengths it gives a polynomial-time \((1+\varepsilon)\)-approximate LP solve. That rules out pretending an LP/rounding output is Track A-hard, but it does not yet rule out exact sparse-spanner search. I’m checking the paper’s two reductions/constructions for a certificate-preserving family whose generated distribution survives exact and construction-aware attacks.
